course: dictionary
lesson: 4378
title: Habitable: can it be lived in?
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Habitable means good enough for people or animals to live in. «A habitable house» has water and heating and is safe, so a family can move in. A house with no roof or water is not habitable yet.
explain: In science, «the habitable zone» is the band around a star where a planet is not too hot and not too cold. Look at the clues around the word: star, planet, too hot and too cold. They show that the meaning is about life, not just houses.
explain: Read «TRAPPIST-1 ... some in its habitable zone.» Ask: what word would fit here? Try "able to support life" in place of habitable, and the sentence still makes sense. Deserts and ice caps are hardly habitable, so the word can also describe places on Earth.
fact: Habitable means good enough for people or animals to live in.
fact: «Habitable zone» is another name for the Goldilocks zone, the band around a star where a planet is not too hot and not too cold.
fact: In the habitable zone, a planet could have liquid water on its surface.
fact: TRAPPIST-1 is a red dwarf, a small, cool, reddish star about 40 light years away.
fact: TRAPPIST-1 has seven rocky planets about the size of Earth, and some of them are in its habitable zone.
exercise: «A habitable house» has water and heating and is safe. What does habitable mean? | Empty and locked for ever | *Good enough for people to live in | Very tall and made of glass | Painted in bright colours
exercise: «Some planets are in its habitable zone.» What is the habitable zone in science? | The dark side of a planet where no light ever reaches | A fast route that rockets use to reach the stars | The hot part of a planet that no one can touch | *The band around a star where a planet is not too hot or too cold
exercise: Dad says deserts and ice caps are hardly habitable. What does hardly habitable suggest? | Full of cities and shops | Perfect for a family picnic | *Very hard for people to live in | Warm and wet all year round
test: Which sentence uses habitable correctly? | The loud band was habitable all through the concert. | The pizza was habitable after lunch. | *The old barn had no roof or water, so it was not habitable.
test: «The astronauts found a planet in the habitable zone.» What does the clue "in the habitable zone" tell you? | The planet is always dark | The planet has no star nearby | The planet is made of sugar | *The planet may be able to support life
exam: Which of these best explains habitable? | *Able to be lived in or to support life, such as «a habitable planet» | Able to open a tin of beans | Able to swim across a lake | Able to run very fast
